In C, `double a[4096][4096]` is summed by two versions of a loop. Which is faster, and why?

```
// A
for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
  for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
    s += a[i][j];
// B
for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
  for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
    s += a[i][j];
```

Assume 64-byte lines, 8-byte doubles and N large. How many cache misses per inner-loop iteration does this have?

```
for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
  for (j = 0; j < N; j++)
    s += a[j][i] * b[i][j];
```
